A||||18||ejayerik||The best way to skin a Fiero||||5||
Z||000000||ejayerik||12-08-2001||09:03 PM||||Hey, I plan to get rid of the fadeing red paint on my 85 2M4 this weekend. What is the best way to sand it down (including the plastic) to primer it. I will leave it primered till the end of the month. I haven't figured out what color I want it yet. The inerior is grey. By the way, I don't have a wet sander and I have the time to do it by hand. Any tips?<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mark Matthews||12-09-2001||12:34 AM||||I have a question along the same lines.  I found the exact matching color by GM at a local parts store.  I was just gonna do a quick light sanding to even the peeled clearcoat off.  Then use like 5 of the spraycans to go over it.  I haven't figured what I'm gonna do with the car yet, so I figure the eas spray for $20 is better than the peeling clearcoat.  Will this work if I just get past the clearcoat? Or do I need to primer over it?  Thanks!<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top<BR>'96 4x4 Tahoe<BR>'74 Wife, not happy about cars!  ;)||||reg||1
Z||000002||fropuff||12-09-2001||01:05 AM||||If there's not too many coats on there, you can usually sand off all the clear until your water turns the paint color.  That means you're through the clear.  Then you use a good grit paper, so the next coat will adhere.  This may work for ya.||||reg||1
Z||000003||skitime||12-09-2001||09:42 AM||||This going to sound crazy but our 86GT had a lot of bad clearcoat and looked awful.  We just took a razor blade and carefully scraped the clearcoat off.  It was easy and went fairly fast.  Afterwards, we used rubbing compound to polish it up.  It was amazing how much better the car looks.  From 10 feet it actually looks good.  This will get us by till we can get it painted.  If I get time I will post before and after pics.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-2.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||ejayerik||12-09-2001||10:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by skitime:<BR><B>This going to sound crazy but our 86GT had a lot of bad clearcoat and looked awful.  We just took a razor blade and carefully scraped the clearcoat off.  It was easy and went fairly fast.  Afterwards, we used rubbing compound to polish it up.  It was amazing how much better the car looks.  From 10 feet it actually looks good.  This will get us by till we can get it painted.  If I get time I will post before and after pics.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You better check the inner pannels of your GT. It may be one of the last off the line. The last two Fieros were produced in Aug of 88 and were given to plant workers. All of the inside pannels (the parts you can't see) were signed by all of the plant employees. They were red, GT, and were T-tops. Check it out and let us know.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ejayerik||12-09-2001||10:25 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B> You better check the inner pannels of your GT. It may be one of the last off the line. The last two Fieros were produced in Aug of 88 and were given to plant workers. All of the inside pannels (the parts you can't see) were signed by all of the plant employees. They were red, GT, and were T-tops. Check it out and let us know.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Duh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"> I thought your GT was an 88. Sorry for the mix-up.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Mark Matthews||12-09-2001||11:05 AM||||SKITIME : That sonds crazy enough that it just might work.  If you could get pictures, that would be awesome.  How long did it take?  And you didn't use any paint huh?  Cool.  How did it hold up under the weather?<P>------------------<BR>Mark Matthews<BR>'57 Chevy BelAir<BR>'70 BMW 2002<BR>'87 GT Fastback T-top<BR>'96 4x4 Tahoe<BR>'74 Wife, not happy about cars!  ;)||||reg||14
Z||000007||skitime||12-09-2001||12:03 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mark Matthews:<BR><B>SKITIME : That sounds crazy enough that it just might work.  If you could get pictures, that would be awesome.  How long did it take?  And you didn't use any paint huh?  Cool.  How did it hold up under the weather?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The paint is holding up fine. It took an evening and Vonnie did it not me.  She used a safety razor blade at a flat angle and most came off quite easily. It just came off like dry powder.  We did wax it so it beads up nice. <P>Here are the before pics.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P3190019-1.JPG">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P3190032-1.JPG">  <P>And here are some after pics.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pc090007.jpg">  <BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Pc090008.jpg">  <P>Good Luck<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by skitime (edited 12-09-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||watts||12-09-2001||12:11 PM||||Before I say anything here...<P>Are there actually any pro auto painters on the board?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Krys||12-09-2001||12:19 PM||||skitime, that 86 GT would look alot better if it wasn't sitting beside that (my) 88 GT there ya know <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> why dont ya go ahead and bring that thing down here and drop it off. hehe.<P>------------------<BR>'88 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe - <A HREF="http://www.fierolinks.f2s.com/index.shtml" TARGET=_blank>Fierolinks</A>||||reg||1
Z||000010||rogergarrison||12-09-2001||07:32 PM||||I own a body shop so I guess Im a pro  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I use the razor method a lot too, but still repaint. You can rub out the  basecoat, but it wont hold up for long. The basecoat color coat is very thin and its flat (no gloss) thats why its cleared in the first place. Sorta like rubbing out a flat black hood on a mustang or roadrunner. It does prob look better than the peeling white though. Just clearing over the red wont work in long run either. It wont stick to old base color long. if you have anypaint left after sanding you should spray some primer around the area that peeled so that the edge wont 'bleed' back thru finished paint. Not that big a deal to paint a hood and trunk lid. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||SKIDMARK||12-09-2001||08:08 PM||||Hey Roger, where can I get a Dupont paint book for cheap? <BR>The paint on a Fiero is "Base coat / clear coat". That is usually Urithane (?spelling) with acrylic clear coat over it. In order to get the clear to stick properly you have to use a 240 or 300 grit wet sand paper and scuff the base coat. GM production line does not wet sand the base coat, therefore, you can usually peel it off with a razor blade. GM had several programs in which they repainted vehicles for free when the clear coat peeled off. <BR>You can get away with just wet sanding the existing finish, as long as it's smooth, and then painting over it using the right kind of paint without any primer. <BR>The best and correct way is to sand down to the color coat, prime, sand, base coat, sand, and clear coat. By the way, the rubber bumpers need a flexitive added to the paint to prevent spider web cracks. The ONLY way to get rid of these cracks is to strip, sand, prime, sand, and repaint the rubber bumpers. <BR>Why do you think a Quality paint job costs so much? It's really labor intensive. And the paint aint cheap either.<BR>I'm not a pro, just read a lot.<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||1
Z||000012||jelly2m8||12-09-2001||08:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ejayerik:<BR><B> You better check the inner pannels of your GT. It may be one of the last off the line. The last two Fieros were produced in Aug of 88 and were given to plant workers. All of the inside pannels (the parts you can't see) were signed by all of the plant employees. They were red, GT, and were T-tops. Check it out and let us know.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR> I read that the last 2 cars were 5 spds, doesn't hurt to chaeck though!<BR><P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://users.eastlink.ca/~acook1/pegged%2001.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||rogergarrison||12-10-2001||06:47 AM||||skid, You have to subscribe to them at paint store. I think mine was $17. Paint store may give you an older one for a particular year or two, or a body shop may give you an old one.  A couple of points though...Gms paint (and others)prob was that they allowed the base colors to dry too long before they got clear, it has to be applied within a certain time. Ive literally redone hundreds of them. You should never sand or even touch the base color till its cleared, especially metallic colors. You will make them streak by dragging the fine silver particles. Flex agent is only necessary to allow you to install the parts without cracking, it evaporates from the paint within a few weeks. If you paint them on the car its perfectly fine to paint them with whole car.....good sanding prep is whats necessary. Thats all I do. Never had any crack that wasnt in another wreck.||||reg||1
Z||000014||SKIDMARK||12-10-2001||01:52 PM||||I agree, you shouldn't wet sand the base coat....my bad. I misunderstood the information I was given.<BR>What I had in mind with the flexitive was on my car, the spider web cracks on the back bumper from being rear-ended before I bought it. All the paint had to be stripped and because I did it off the car, I used a flexitive to be able to install them without causing more cracks in the paint.<BR>The Dupont book I was refering to gives step by step for the use of each of their products. You know, the application information they used to put on the can label for free. I was quoted over $100 by the paint store for this book. If it's only $17, maybe I should find another paint store to deal with.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||rogergarrison||12-10-2001||05:10 PM||||no prob. They give you all the application instruction pamphlets free for each of the products. If they dont, go to another supplier. They had to do that because EPA put so much BS and warnings, there isnt room on the label anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000016||ejayerik||12-10-2001||08:46 PM||||Ok, let me get this strait. I can NOT sand the plastic? All I want to do is get the paint off. There is no clearcoat left exept on the rear bumper. I want to get it down to the original gelcoat and then primer it. Can I do this via wet sand? Any suggestions about things I should watch out for. It seems this disscusion went from the paint on my car to the color coat book. Nice GT BTW.||||reg||1
Z||000017||rogergarrison||12-10-2001||11:51 PM||||Yes you can wet sand the plastic. You can sand off all the paint which is the best idea, #400 grit or finer. Reprime it, sand the primer. Then basecoat and clear. your done. If you want to make it really slick, after a couple days wet sand with #1000 grit and rub it out. DONT MASK with newspaper !!<BR>urathane goes right thru it, and youll mess up your good paint.If your just painting the hood and trunk, you wont have any problems with runs either, even if you never done it before.||||reg||1
Z||000018||ejayerik||12-11-2001||12:58 AM||||Hey thanks. I finaly got a strait answer. BTW, I plan to sand the whole car. The idiot that owned it before me whent to hell trying to fix the paint with rubbing compound. It is really beond help. Time to strip it all off. Good think I didn't listen to a buddy and use aircraft paint remover. Would have reeked havic on the plastic. No newspaper huh. How about masking tape. A whole lot of it.||||reg||2
